\import{fcap-macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\tag{fcap}
\tag{spin}
\tag{clifford}

\taxon{Proposition}
\title{Eichler transvections have additive Spin lifts}

\p{Let #{K} be a field in which #{2} is invertible, let #{V} be a
#{K}-space, and let #{Q} be nondegenerate. Write #{B_Q} for the unhalved
polar form and #{\widehat v=\iota_Q(v)} for a Clifford generator. Suppose

##{Q(u)=0,\qquad B_Q(u,w)=0.}

The Eichler transvection and its Clifford lift are

##{\begin{aligned}
E_{u,w}(x)
 &=x+B_Q(x,u)w-B_Q(x,w)u-Q(w)B_Q(x,u)u,\\
L_{u,w}&=1+\widehat w\widehat u.
\end{aligned}}

Then #{L_{u,w}\in\Spin(Q)}, its Spin action is #{E_{u,w}}, and

##{L_{u,w}^{-1}=\operatorname{rev}(L_{u,w})=1-\widehat w\widehat u.}

The formal transformation formula already makes sense over a commutative
ring and module, without nondegeneracy or invertible #{2}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.transvection_apply}
\leanref/tauceti{QuadraticMap.transvection_mem_specialOrthogonalGroup}.
The actual Spin lift uses the field, invertible-two, and nondegeneracy
hypotheses stated above
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.coe_spinTransvection}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.coe_spinToSpecialOrthogonal_spinTransvection}.}

\p{Indeed, put #{N=\widehat w\widehat u}. The Clifford relations give

##{\begin{gathered}
N^2=0,\qquad \operatorname{rev}(N)=-N,\\
N\widehat x-\widehat xN
 =B_Q(x,u)\widehat w-B_Q(x,w)\widehat u,\\
N\widehat xN=Q(w)B_Q(x,u)\widehat u.
\end{gathered}}

Thus expansion of #{(1+N)\widehat x(1-N)} gives exactly the displayed
formula, including its negative quadratic term, and proves reverse norm one.
Actual membership in the vector-generated carrier is a separate point. If
#{Q(w)\ne0}, the explicit factorization is

##{1+\widehat w\widehat u
=\widehat{\frac{w}{Q(w)}}\,
 \widehat{\bigl(w+Q(w)u\bigr)},}

and the two quadratic values multiply to #{1}. If #{Q(w)=0} and
#{w\notin Ku}, nondegeneracy supplies #{x\perp u} with
#{b=B_Q(w,x)\ne0}. For

##{a=x+\frac{b-Q(x)}{b}w}

one has #{Q(a)=b}, #{Q(w+a)=2b}, and
#{L_{u,w}=L_{u,w+a}L_{u,-a}}, reducing to the nonisotropic case. If
#{w\in Ku}, the lift is #{1}. This is the algebraic carrier argument behind
the linked formal construction; it needs no finite-dimensionality.}

\p{For #{w,w'\in u^\perp}, the cross term
#{(\widehat w\widehat u)(\widehat{w'}\widehat u)} vanishes. Hence

##{L_{u,w+w'}=L_{u,w}L_{u,w'},\qquad
L_{u,-w}=L_{u,w}^{-1},\qquad
L_{u,w+cu}=L_{u,w}.}

The same equations hold after projection to the Eichler transformations.
Thus the lifts form a homomorphism

##{u^{\perp}/Ku\longrightarrow\Spin(Q)}

which commutes with the projection to #{SO(Q)}; when #{u\ne0}, it is
injective
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinTransvection_add}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinTransvection_add_smul}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinToSpecialOrthogonal_comp_spinTransvectionHom}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinTransvectionHom_injective}.
The domain is #{u^{\perp}/Ku}, not all of #{V/Ku}; for #{u=0} the family is
trivial. If #{V} is additionally finite-dimensional, these transformations
have trivial spinor norm, as predicted by \ref{fcap-0023}
\leanref/tauceti{CliffordAlgebra.spinorNorm_transvection}.}

\p{Chevalley treats the common totally singular case
#{Q(u)=Q(w)=B_Q(u,w)=0}: his finite exponential is #{1+\widehat w\widehat u}
and his conjugation formula is the displayed action with its quadratic term
zero
\citet{III.1.7, pp. 73--74}{chevalley1954algebraic}.
This passage does not state the arbitrary-#{Q(w)} formula. Meinrenken's
quantization of the bivector #{w\wedge u} is #{\widehat w\widehat u}; his
commutator theorem and exponential Spin comparison recover the full formula
over finite-dimensional nondegenerate real or complex spaces
\citet{Section 2.2.10, Theorem 2.1 and Proposition 2.12, pp. 39--40}{meinrenken2013clifford}
\citet{Proposition 3.2, pp. 53--54}{meinrenken2013clifford}.
Here his symmetric form is the half-polar form
#{B_M=b_Q=B_Q/2}. Since #{N^2=0}, both the Clifford and vector exponentials
truncate, and the written polynomial calculation fixes the normalization and
extends the formula to the stated general field. No claim that every Spin
element is one exponential is made.}
